;    1  ! fcalc: polynomials, as vectors of their coefficients.
;    2  !
;    3  ! A polynomial is a vector of its coefficients, the highest power's first:
;    4  ! [1, -3, 2] is x^2 - 3x + 2. (MATLAB and Octave write them so, and poly
;    5  ! and roots undo each other.) Three functions, in FCMATRIX's table:
;    6  !
;    7  !     roots(p)      the roots of p, a column (FCMATRIX sorts them)
;    8  !     poly(r)       the polynomial whose roots are the elements of r, with
;    9  !                   leading coefficient 1, a row
;   10  !     polyval(p, x) p's value at x, at each element of x
;   11  !
;   12  ! The roots are found by the Durand-Kerner method (also called Weierstrass's
;   13  ! method), which improves every root at once. For a polynomial with leading
;   14  ! coefficient 1 and roots r1, ..., rn,
;   15  !
;   16  !     p(z) = (z - r1)(z - r2) ... (z - rn),
;   17  !
;   18  ! so if z1, ..., zn are guesses, rk is zk less p(zk) divided by the product
;   19  ! of (zk - zj) over the other guesses j, exactly, when the other guesses
;   20  ! are right. The method makes that step for each guess in turn, using the
;   21  ! guesses already improved (as the Gauss-Seidel method for linear systems
;   22  ! does), until no guess moves. Each simple root is then found to the
;   23  ! machine's precision, quadratically; a root of multiplicity m only to
;   24  ! about 1/m of the digits, which is all any method can promise, since a
;   25  ! change of one in the last bit of a coefficient moves such a root that
;   26  ! far. A step of Newton's method on each root (z less p(z)/p'(z)) then
;   27  ! polishes it, if it makes p(z) smaller.
;   28  !
;   29  ! The guesses start on a circle that holds every root, by Fujiwara's bound
;   30  ! (every root of z^n + a1 z^(n-1) + ... + an lies within twice the largest
;   31  ! of |a1|, |a2|^(1/2), ..., |an/2|^(1/n)), spread around it with an offset
;   32  ! so no guess starts on a line of symmetry of the roots (where the
;   33  ! iteration could stay, unable to leave the real axis for a complex pair).
;   34  !
;   35  ! Zero coefficients at the end are roots at zero, taken out first, so the
;   36  ! roots of x^3 - x^2 are 0, 0, and 1 exactly. Every operation is guarded
;   37  ! (FCVALUES' C_ADD, C_SUB, C_MUL, C_DIV), so nothing traps.
;   38  module fcpoly
;   39    use fckinds
;   40    use fcmessages
;   41    use fcvalues
;   42    use fcarrays
;   43    implicit none
;   44    private
;   45
;   46    public :: quantity
;   47    public :: polynomial_roots, polynomial_of, polynomial_value
;   48
;   49    ! How many sweeps the iteration may make, and how many in a row may
;   50    ! fail to improve on its best before it stops.
;   51    integer, parameter :: sweep_limit = 500, stale_limit = 20
;   52
;   53  contains

